    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Epiretinal prostheses are designed to restore vision in people blinded by photoreceptor degenerative diseases, by directly activating retinal ganglion cells (RGCs) using an electrode array implanted on the retina. However, in present-day clinical devices, large current spread to distant grounds often results in the activation of many cells. Achieving greater acuity of artificial vision may require the ability to control RGC activity more finely. In this study, the ability to selectively activate individual RGCs using local return stimulation was evaluated. A custom multi-electrode array was used to simultaneously stimulate and record from hundreds of RGCs in isolated primate retina. Stimulation with a single electrode and a distant ground was compared to stimulation in which the ground was provided by neighboring electrodes. Local return stimulation allowed activation of cells near the central electrode while avoiding activation of cells farther away. This made it possible to more reliably activate a target cell without activating a non-target cell. These results suggest that a device that restricts the electric field through local returns could optimize activation of target neurons at cellular resolution, potentially improving the quality of artificial vision.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
